  "question": {
    "text": "If $\\sin ^{2} \\theta+\\sin ^{2} \\phi=1 / 2, \\cos ^{2} \\theta+\\cos ^{2} \\phi=3 / 2,$ then $\\cos ^{2}(\\theta-\\phi)$ is equal to"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "$3 / 8$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$5 / 8$",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "$3 / 4$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$5 / 4$",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "Using cosine formula \r\n$2 \\sin (\\theta+\\phi) \\cos (\\theta-\\phi)=1 / 2$ ...(i)\r\n$2 \\cos (\\theta+\\phi) \\cos (\\theta-\\phi)=3 / 2$ ...(ii)\r\nSquaring (1) and (2) and then adding $\\cos ^{2}(\\theta-\\phi)=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{5}{2} \\Rightarrow \\cos ^{2}(\\theta-\\phi)=\\frac{5}{8}$"
  }
}